+## Proxy and Catalog Recovery
+
+- Check the configured `unreal-mcp` or `unreal-mcp-proxy` connection. A proxy preserves native Unreal tool definitions and adds no replacement discovery/dispatch API.
+- `unreal_mcp_status` describes the proxy's initialized upstream session; it does not test current reachability. Visible tools can come from a cached catalog.
+- Without a stored catalog or upstream session, only `unreal_mcp_status` may be visible. Recovery emits `notifications/tools/list_changed`; the client must fetch `tools/list` again.
+- If the client keeps an old/status-only catalog after Unreal is reachable, reconnect/reload that connection, or restart Codex when no reload action exists. Keep the existing proxy during normal editor recovery; do not add a duplicate direct connection.
+- Killing a proxy closes its client STDIO connection. A new unrelated process cannot repair `Transport closed`; reconnect the client. Stop an active shared proxy only when explicitly authorized.
+- After recovery, execute a read-only Unreal tool call before any mutation. A status flag, HTTP probe, or cached tool list is insufficient.
+
+For persistent failures, inspect the editor startup log, configured endpoint, and proxy stderr. With multiple editors, identify which process owns the configured port and confirm the target project. Follow the local engine's `Extras/Proxy/README.md` for supported transport/configuration; not all engine installations include it.
